\section{Scenario Family Parameters}\label{app:A}

\paragraph{Designed family.}
36 scenarios across 5 categories, each at 3 difficulties (108 configurations): Reconnaissance (8), Tracking \& Surveillance (8), Interception \& Response (8), Zone Control (6), Emergency Response (6). Estimated intrinsic couplings: 1.2 / 1.8 / 2.2 / 2.8 / 2.0 respectively. The inter-domain dependency strength $I \in [0,1]$ composes temporal dependency ($+0.3$), spatial co-location ($+0.3$), and information flow ($+0.3$), capped at 1.

\paragraph{Admitted interception-engagement scenarios.}
Table~\ref{tab:scenparams} lists the 14 admitted scenarios of the interception-engagement category, exported verbatim from the frozen scenario packs released with the benchmark; parameter tables for the remaining categories will be exported from their packs upon admission under the same gates.

\input{tables/tab_scenario_params}

\paragraph{Headroom trend within the admitted family.}
Across the 14 scenarios, each layered stack's gain over the best monolithic baseline (per-scenario episode means, under the no-intelligence regime of the main results) declines with baseline strength: Spearman $\rho = -0.50$ for LLM+Rule ($p = 0.07$) and $\rho = -0.68$ for LLM+RL ($p = 0.008$), $n = 14$; excluding IE-03, $\rho = -0.37$ ($p = 0.21$) and $\rho = -0.59$ ($p = 0.03$). Five of the eight negative cells fall in the four strongest-baseline scenarios. This is an observational trend on a proxy composite rather than a controlled headroom manipulation, but it is the direction the layering law's headroom term predicts.

\paragraph{Scenario-family admission calibration.}
To widen coverage beyond the interception-engagement category, an admission campaign re-calibrated 30 scenarios into the headroom window $[0.40, 0.85]$ on the best pure baseline, 5 seeds $\times$ 60 episodes per scenario per batch (batches independent, never pooled). The campaign's category encoding refines the design-level categories above: AD (adversarial-defense, 6), ER (emergency-response, 6), REC (reconnaissance, 10), TRK (tracking, 8); ledger and design-family counts differ by construction---the design-level family states 36 canonical scenarios, the admission ledger tracks 55 entries including re-calibrated variants, and the 30 confirmed scenarios are the subset whose 5-seed re-calibration completed. Under the composite scoring the campaign reaches 15/30 scenarios inside the window (7/30 before re-calibration), with AD (5), ER (4), and REC (4) each contributing $\geq 3$ in-window scenarios---meeting the D2 gate across three categories---while TRK contributes 2 (MD-TRK-005's re-calibrated variant reaches 0.557 in a single-seed check; its 5-seed confirmation is pending). The full admission ledger covers 55 scenarios (DF prior ratings on 44; D2: 16 pass / 19 fail / 20 pending). Re-calibration used declared scenario parameters only (AD: protected-zone advance along the threat axis, $x=520$: $1.000 \rightarrow x=900$: $0.45$--$0.63$; ER: response-zone extension, $+60$ m: $0.914 \rightarrow +70$ m: $0.601$; TRK: observer retreat / blue-target advance), with three documented costs: floor scripts (indiscriminate/naive) become the best pure baselines in several scenarios (difficulty from geometry, not decision quality); several metrics are cliff-shaped (gap 0/1, arrival $1.0 \rightarrow 0.23$) with no continuous gradient; and the feasible difficulty band is narrow (MD-TRK-002 $=0.393$, MD-AD-001 $=0.443$ straddle the window edge).

\paragraph{DF prior-rating validation.}
The 6-dimension decision-form prior is a 6-point checklist (0/1 per item): (1) cross-domain allocation required; (2) intent inference or deception handling required; (3) protocol/compliance reasoning required; (4) open-language briefing interpretable by an LLM rather than a numeric table; (5) spatial micro-control is \emph{not} the sole bottleneck; (6) a myopic per-unit greedy script falls short of a rule baseline. Items (1)--(4) are semantic-decision forms the LLM handles well; item (5) excludes geometric-micro-control scenarios; item (6) is the operationalization of DF. We rated the prior on 7 paired scenarios and tested its correlation with measured layered gain. Spearman $\rho = -0.0092$ ($p = 0.997$), criterion ($\rho > 0.5$, $p < 0.05$) not met. The test is uninformative rather than falsifying: layered gains range only over $[-0.030, +0.086]$ across the 7 scenarios, and detecting $\rho > 0.5$ at that range would require on the order of 50 scenarios. The scenario-family calibration that standardizes pure baselines into the window also compresses the layered-gain variance the correlation needs. We therefore do not claim predictive validity for the prior rating; DF is supported as a design-side gate plus the anchor pair (grid: DF fails $\rightarrow$ layering does not pay; high-fidelity: DF holds $\rightarrow$ layering pays) and the mechanism probe (Section~6.9), and mid-range quantitative validation is left to the enlarged family.

\section{Polling API Specification}\label{app:B}

The distributed platform separates simulation (server side) from inference (client side). The interface is a RESTful HTTP API exchanging JSON documents; no model weights, code, or environment state beyond the API surface ever crosses the boundary.

\paragraph{Endpoints.}
(i) \texttt{POST /session} --- create a session, return \texttt{session\_id} plus a capability manifest; (ii) \texttt{GET /session/\{id\}/briefing} --- fetch the current natural-language mission briefing; (iii) \texttt{GET /session/\{id\}/state} --- fetch the rendered observation (layout fixed by scenario configuration: JSON default, natural-language variant available for the modality study); (iv) \texttt{POST /session/\{id\}/decisions} --- submit a decision (goal plan for the planner role, action for the executor role); (v) \texttt{GET /session/\{id\}/events} --- poll termination and event messages; (vi) \texttt{DELETE /session/\{id\}} --- close and archive.

\paragraph{JSON payloads.}
A decision payload is \texttt{\small\{"goals":[\allowbreak\{"unit":<id>\allowbreak,"goal":<goal-id>\allowbreak,"params":<param-object>\}]\allowbreak, "clock":<tick>\}}; the server replies with \texttt{\small\{"accepted":[...]\allowbreak, "rejected":[...]\allowbreak, "reason":<code>\}}. Goal vocabulary, parameter ranges, and rejection codes are published in the frozen API manifest shipped with the benchmark.

\paragraph{Session resumption and integrity.}
Simulation advances asynchronously of inference: a 60\,s LLM deliberation does not block the server, and sessions are resumable from the last acknowledged tick. Resumption was verified under 120\,s injected decision latency (load test, 20 parallel sessions, 0 desynchronizations). Integrity measures bound the trust model: per-session rate limits on decision submissions, action-pattern consistency checks (rejected actions outside the manifest vocabulary or parameter ranges), and manifest-hash verification on session creation.

\section{Tactic Templates and Strength Knobs}\label{app:C}

The scripted opponent deploys through tactic templates---saturation pressure, multi-axis feint, escorted advance, dispersed probing---each parameterized by calibrated strength knobs. Knobs adjust wave cadence, feint ratio, escort count, and probing dispersion; the knob sweep doubles as the D2 headroom-calibration instrument (baseline success-rate operating window $[40\%, 85\%]$ for the admitted suite). Templates are deterministic functions of the scenario clock and the role-standardized briefing channel; they never read the evaluated agent's private state.

\section{Attribution Validity Guarantees}\label{app:D}

Five pre-registered checks guard the counterfactual-replay decomposition: (i) identical interface definitions across counterfactuals; (ii) deterministic dynamics with fixed seeds; (iii) replay consistency---own-trace replay reproduces original performance; (iv) monotonicity---qualitatively worse executors yield larger $\Delta E$; (v) reference-component quality independently validated on single-domain subtasks.

\paragraph{Executor-effectiveness precheck.}
Attribution requires an executor that responds to goals. When the executor sits at a performance floor, planner differences are masked; attribution is therefore gated with an executor responsiveness test (goal-conditioned vs.\ monolithic SR gap) and floor effects are reported explicitly.

\paragraph{Anchored-fault extension.}
To sharpen localization, each synthetic fault is anchored at a \emph{critical opportunity} in a pre-matched trajectory prefix (state fingerprint matching the clean run up to injection), with injection reach, local consequence, and endgame effect reported separately. The layer of first appearance is localized at the goal/action recording boundary in 20/20 paired seeds under four gates: integrity-and-replay (fault injection does not break trace replay), critical-exposure (the anchored opportunity is genuinely decision-relevant), matched-prefix (fingerprints align up to injection), and boundary-localization (the recording boundary discriminates the layer). These are known synthetic faults on a rule-planner base under matched prefixes---calibration of the recording-and-replay pipeline, not blind natural-fault attribution.

\paragraph{Determinism scope.}
Replay assumes a deterministic system, satisfied by the grid. The real-stream counterfactual validates the decomposition on the \emph{live} LLM planning stream replay-gated, with the goal recording boundary---where planner output enters the interface---fixed by instrumentation. On the nondeterministic high-fidelity endpoint, the diagnosis instead uses the prompt-level audit and the three-part stability criterion. Counterfactual executor substitution freezes the original goal sequence by construction, so faults that degrade the executor itself (rather than its goals) are outside the measured scope of the replay-gated attribution.

\section{Layered Metric Suite}\label{app:E}

\paragraph{Planning-attributable.}
Mission Understanding Score (behavior--priority alignment); Resource Allocation Efficiency (vs.\ MILP optimum); Adaptability Score (time-to-recover after instruction switches, normalized by severity); Cross-Domain Coordination Score (handover success and timing across domains, track-maintenance ratios); Opponent Intent Inference Score with sub-metrics Anticipation Lead Time, Feint Detection Rate, Target Prediction Accuracy.

\paragraph{Execution-attributable.}
Task Success Rate; Time/Energy Efficiency; Collision \& Safety Score; Tracking Accuracy; friction statistics (fuel, interference events, packet loss, obstacle collisions).

\paragraph{Composite.}
$\mathrm{Score}_{\mathrm{total}} = \alpha \cdot \mathrm{Score}_{\mathrm{planning}} + \beta \cdot \mathrm{Score}_{\mathrm{execution}}$ (default $0.5/0.5$). Aggregation uses paired statistics (Wilcoxon signed-rank, sign tests, bootstrap CIs) with seeds paired across systems. All raw measures are dumped per-episode as released traces.

