\section{Deception Design and Discrimination Statistics}\label{app:G}

\paragraph{Feature indistinguishability.}
In the grid, 50\% of transports are feints whose numeric features (position, speed, signature) are indistinguishable from genuine targets; the only tell is heading kinematics visible through repeated semantic contacts, never in the local grid encoding. On the high-fidelity suite, the pre-registered IA protocol measures a numeric-feature discriminator at or below 60\% (rule) and the LLM-based intent model at or above 85\% on feint-vs-real; the outcome is reported as a diagnostic and mechanism probe, not an acceptance gate (Section~6.9).

\paragraph{Observed feint exposure on the grid (six-arm batch).}
Over seeds 501--510, the rule-rule stack observed 4 feints and hit all 4 observed; rule-rl observed 1; llm-rl observed 1; pure-rl observed 2; the LLM stacks observed 0 feints. Feint hits are a behavioral proxy, not detection accuracy: with $\leq$1 feint observed per LLM stack in 10 seeds, we do \emph{not} claim an autonomous feint-recognition rate; feint handling contributes only where feints are actually observed and engaged.

